WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.512
This query creates a diagnosis summary materialized view.

00:00:03.512 --> 00:00:06.137
The result has one row per diagnosis.

00:00:06.137 --> 00:00:10.192
It stores admission count, distinct patient count, average stay, and a refresh timestamp.

00:00:10.192 --> 00:00:15.687
The refresh timestamp documents when the snapshot was generated.

00:00:15.687 --> 00:00:19.277
It does not update every time someone reads the materialized view.

00:00:19.277 --> 00:00:23.647
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:23.647 --> 00:00:27.332
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:27.332 --> 00:00:30.932
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:30.932 --> 00:00:36.407
In a real setting such as a governance project limiting which columns different users can access,

00:00:36.407 --> 00:00:41.962
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:41.962 --> 00:00:43.947
before discussing syntax.

00:00:43.947 --> 00:00:49.520
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
